Here is a breakdown of how your 2023 taxes would generally be handled based on the information provided:

### 1. Standard Deduction vs. Itemizing
**You should take the Standard Deduction.** 
For the 2023 tax year, the standard deduction for a single filer is **$13,850**. 
To itemize, your eligible personal deductions would need to exceed $13,850. Your listed personal deductions are:
*   Charitable donation: $650
*   State and local taxes (SALT): (Not provided, but usually capped at $10,000)
Since your itemizable deductions are likely well below $13,850, the standard deduction is much more beneficial for you. Note that because you are taking the standard deduction, your $650 charitable donation to the Austin Food Bank will not reduce your taxable income (the temporary pandemic-era rule allowing non-itemizers to deduct charity expired after 2021).

### 2. Freelance Income & Deductions (Schedule C)
Your $19,700 from 1099-NEC work makes you a sole proprietor in the eyes of the IRS. You can deduct business expenses against this income to lower your self-employment tax and income tax.
*   **Unreimbursed Work Supplies ($1,120):** If these supplies were for your *freelance* editing/proofreading work, you can deduct them fully on your Schedule C. *(Note: If these were for your W-2 job at Summit Financial, they are no longer deductible under current federal tax law).*

### 3. The Home Office Deduction
Because you have freelance income, you can claim a home office deduction **if** the 95 sq ft room is used *exclusively and regularly* for your freelance business. (If you use it for your W-2 job, it does not qualify). You have two ways to calculate this:
*   **Simplified Method:** $5 per square foot. (95 sq ft x $5 = **$475** deduction). This is easiest and requires less record-keeping.
*   **Actual Expenses Method:** You calculate the percentage of your home used for business (95 sq ft / 810 sq ft = **~11.73%**). You can deduct 11.73% of your rent ($1,950 x 12 = $23,400 total rent x 11.73% = **~$2,744**), plus 11.73% of utilities, renter's insurance, and internet. This yields a much higher deduction but requires you to fill out Form 8829.

### 4. "Above-the-Line" Deductions (Adjustments to Income)
You can take these deductions even if you take the standard deduction:
*   **Student Loan Interest:** You paid $2,900, but the IRS caps this deduction at **$2,500** per year. You can deduct the maximum $2,500, which will lower your Adjusted Gross Income (AGI).
*   **Self-Employment Tax Deduction:** You are allowed to deduct half of the self-employment taxes you pay on your freelance income.

### 5. Forms and Worksheets You Will Need
When filing, you will need the following federal forms:
*   **Form 1040:** The main individual income tax return.
*   **Schedule 1:** To report your business income, student loan interest deduction, and the deductible part of your self-employment tax.
*   **Schedule 2:** To report your self-employment tax.
*   **Schedule B:** To report your Interest ($1,120) and Dividends ($510). *(Required because your combined interest and dividends exceed $1,500).*
*   **Schedule C:** To report your $19,700 freelance income and deduct your work supplies.
*   **Schedule SE:** To calculate the self-employment tax on your freelance profit.
*   **Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home):** Only needed if you choose the "Actual Expenses" method for your home office instead of the simplified method.

*Make sure you have your W-2, 1099-NECs, 1099-INT (Ally Bank), 1099-DIV (Vanguard), and 1098-E (AES Student Loans) on hand when you file!*

