*   **Self-Employment Tax:** You will owe self-employment tax (Medicare and Social Security) on your net freelance profit. You will calculate this on **Schedule SE**. You get to deduct half of this tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.

### 3. The Home Office Deduction
Because you use a specific area of your home *exclusively* and *regularly* for your freelance business, you can take the home office deduction against your 1099-NEC income. There are two ways to calculate this:
*   **Simplified Method:** $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft. (95 sq ft × $5 = **$475 deduction**).
*   **Actual Expenses Method:** You calculate the percentage of your home used for business (95 sq ft / 810 sq ft = **11.73%**). You can deduct 11.73% of your rent and utilities. 
    *   *Rent calculation:* $1,950/month × 12 months = $23,400/year. 
    *   *Deduction:* 11.73% of $23,400 = **$2,744.82**.
*   **Verdict:** The Actual Expenses method gives you a significantly higher deduction. You will use **Form 8829** to calculate and claim this.

### 4. Student Loan Interest
You paid $2,900 in student loan interest. The IRS allows you to deduct up to **$2,500** of student loan interest as an "above-the-line" deduction (meaning you can take it even if you take the standard deduction). 
*   **Important Note on Phase-outs:** For 2023, this deduction begins to phase out for single filers with a Modified Adjusted Gross Income (MAGI) of $75,000 and is completely eliminated at a MAGI of $90,000. Because your combined W-2 and 1099 income (minus business deductions) puts your MAGI right around or slightly above $90,000, your student loan interest deduction may be significantly reduced or completely phased out.

