Your total itemized deductions would be around $10,650, which is significantly lower than the $13,850 standard deduction. 

### 2. Freelance Income & Home Office (Schedule C)
Your $19,700 in 1099-NEC income means you are considered self-employed for that portion of your work. You will report this on **Schedule C**. This is where you can deduct your business expenses to lower your taxable self-employment income:
*   **Unreimbursed Work Supplies ($1,120):** If these supplies were used for your *freelance* editing/proofreading work, you can deduct the full $1,120 as a business expense on Schedule C. (If they were for your W-2 job, they are not deductible).
*   **Home Office Deduction:** Because you use the 95 sq ft room *exclusively* and regularly for your freelance business, you qualify for this deduction. You have two choices:
